     "Yes, it should.  I added a note to fix this in the next manual.\r<br>\r<br><a href=\"https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/issues/1081#issuecomment-67561559\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/issues/1081%23issuecomment-67561559&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792465410359000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2o0dbSUoBLRRzviZXHzzhy\">https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/issues/1081#issuecomment-67561559</a>\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Dec 17, 2014, at 7:57 PM, Cody Ross &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ctr...@ucdavis.edu</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; On page 248 of version 2.5.0-4 I see:\r<br>&gt; The truncation has the same effect as the following direct update to the accumulated\r<br>&gt; log probability (see the subsection of Section 24.3 contrasting log probability increment\r<br>&gt; and sampling statements for more information).\r<br>&gt; increment_log_prob(normal_log(y, 0, 1));\r<br>&gt; increment_log_prob(-(1 - log(normal_cdf(-0.5, 0, 1))));\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Should it read instead as?:\r<br>&gt; increment_log_prob(normal_log(y, 0, 1));\r<br>&gt; increment_log_prob(-   log(1 - normal_cdf(-0.5, 0, 1) )  );\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I notice a bug when I use the first notation, but I&#39;m not entirely confident the way I show is correct. Any ideas?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
